From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:10:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll, wineserver: tear down a pseudo-process another process
 terminated

A child that another process terminates (TerminateProcess from the
session root, run 20261019T161557) never runs process_exit_wrapper: the
server kills its threads, they end through abort_thread, and abort_thread
calls abort_process only when nb_threads, one count for the whole task,
reaches zero. So its master socket, fd cache, JIT-pool ranges and tail
carves, and 0154's view release were never given back; SocialClubHelper
pid 0x284 kept about 1 GB as an `exited` [proc-mem] row. FEX's ThreadTerm
runs only from the PE side's NtTerminateThread, so none of its threads
freed its rpmalloc heap, call-return stack or L1 cache either. And 11 of
pid 0x1b4's threads, blocked in an x86-64 program's in-process wait
(NtWaitForAlertByThreadId), were never ended: the abort wakes only i386
waits and retried them for a minute.

The server now reports every ended process (finish_process_tracing, from
process_killed). At each thread creation and reclamation, on a thread of
another process, ntdll checks every reported child that has not exited by
itself; once each of its threads in teb_list has finished or is parked it
tears it down, once (pp_proc_end.h): it takes the master socket slot
atomically and closes it, recording a dead child, releases the fd cache
and the JIT-pool ranges, and deletes the views 0154 deletes. Unlike a
self-exit it also deletes the process's views in FEX's band: they belong
to its own FEX instance, whose rpmalloc heaps no other process frees into,
and no thread of it runs. Each teardown logs a `[proc-end] peb=... pid=...
killed: freed image= private= spans= callret= l1= band_other= pool= MB,
kept ... MB; N finished, M parked thread(s)` line. The finished and parked
TEBs then lose their PEB pointer and TlsExpansionSlots (the PE side never
freed them; virtual_clear_tls_index writes through it), which keeps them
out of every later per-process walk. The fixed-base main image is not
retired, as that needs a server call on the process's own thread;
ios_jit_reclaim_process marks it dead as before (ml983).

A killed thread of a process being terminated as a whole is parked, not
ended, when its state proves it blocked in the futex wait of
NtWaitForAlertByThreadId, holding nothing: the instruction before its pc
is svc #0x80 with x16 = 544 (__ulock_wait2, which os_sync_wait_on_address
makes on iOS 17.4 and later) or 515 (__ulock_wait) and x0
UL_COMPARE_AND_WAIT,
in_alert_wait is set and it is not an i386 wait, FEX's TEB lock stamps
(WritePriorityMutex shared and write depth, CodeBufferWriteMutex,
CodeInvalidationMutex read hold, RWX read hold) and InSyscallCallback are
clear, it holds no uninterrupted section or tracked host mutex (a new
mirror of server_ios.c's section depth in its thread data: virtual_mutex,
fd_cache_mutex, the WoW64 registry lock), and it does not own its PEB's
loader lock. rpmalloc's heap lock is a spin lock whose holder only maps
memory and never alert-waits. The abort then leaves it suspended for ever
and stops retrying; one `[proc-end] parked tid=... wait=...` line each.
Anything not shown is refused, logged once as `[proc-end] not parked
tid=...: <reason> (insn= x16= x0= host= fex=)`, and the thread runs on as
before. Nothing resumes it:
the server's thread object is gone, and every other suspend in the
runtime is paired with its own resume. A parked thread keeps its TEB, its
stacks (runtime views) and every view one of its saved registers (x0-x28,
fp, lr, sp, pc, and the guest's 16 GPRs and rip) points into; of its
process's views in FEX's band only the L1 caches go, as the FEX state
that names them stays.

Linear: PLA-416
Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-19-rdr2-loading-memory.md
Offered-upstream: no; pseudo-processes in one Mach task are the iOS port's
---
